<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    TLS trust model (ticket §9.5; corrected by issue #961 — the file used to
    describe a KeyChain-import flow that was never implemented).

    Production: system CAs only, cleartext forbidden. Self-signed and
    private-CA certificates are NOT trusted by a release build — there is no
    `installCustomCertificate`/KeyChain-import path, and this config's trust
    anchor is `system` only (the `user` anchor exists solely in the debug
    source set below, which never ships in a release APK). Since minSdk 26,
    Android does not trust user-installed CAs by default, so installing the
    operator's cert via Settings does not help a release build either way. A
    self-hosted deployment the Android app can reach needs a certificate
    chaining to a CA the OS already trusts (e.g. a public ACME issuer such as
    Let's Encrypt in front of whatever reverse proxy terminates TLS, per
    docs/deployment.md's "How the Docker Setup Works").

    This is the config a RELEASE build actually uses. Debug builds don't use
    this file at all — the debug source set fully replaces it with
    app/src/debug/res/xml/network_security_config.xml, which is where the
    debug-only <debug-overrides> trusting user-installed CAs lives. Do not add
    a <debug-overrides> block here: lint flags it as a duplicate against the
    debug variant's own block ("Expecting at most 1 <debug-overrides>"), since
    the debug build's resource model merges both files.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
